# Line endings.
#
# This project is developed on Windows and deployed to Linux, which is exactly
# the situation where line endings stop being cosmetic. A shell script stored
# with CRLF fails on the server as:
#
#     ./docker-entrypoint.sh: not found
#
# ...because the kernel reads the shebang as "/bin/sh\r" and looks for an
# interpreter with a carriage return in its name. The error names the script,
# not the real problem, so it costs an hour the first time you meet it.
#
# Today the repository happens to hold LF because this machine has
# core.autocrlf=true. That is a per-machine setting, not a property of the
# project: clone it somewhere configured differently and the next commit can
# introduce CRLF. These rules put the guarantee in the repository instead.

# Normalise everything to LF inside the repository by default.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that MUST be LF in the working copy too, because they are executed by
# Linux or parsed by a container at runtime.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.cron          text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf
.env*.example   text eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touch these. Git's newline conversion corrupts a font or
# an image silently — the file still "exists", it just no longer renders.
*.png           binary
*.ico           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.webp          binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.otf           binary
*.ttf           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.gz            binary
*.zip           binary
